# Line endings are not cosmetic here: every file in this repository is a script that some runtime
# executes directly, and two different runtimes disagree about what a line ends with.

# Force LF for shell scripts (CRLF breaks the shebang on Windows Git Bash: the kernel reads
# "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r" and looks for an interpreter literally named "bash\r").
*.sh text eol=lf

# The test harness is shell too, and it is run on Linux and macOS in CI. A .bats or .bash file that
# reaches those machines with CRLF fails at `load`, with an error that names a carriage return.
# Found on 2026-09-16 running this suite in a Linux container (#31245 QA round 2).
*.bats text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# And the Windows scripts are the mirror image. cmd.exe and PowerShell are CRLF runtimes, and
# nothing here pinned them, so what a customer received depended on the core.autocrlf setting of
# whoever last checked the file out - LF with autocrlf=false, CRLF with autocrlf=true. Two
# different files from one commit.
#
# This matters more since #31245 than it did before: codex-hook.cmd is on the hot path of EVERY
# MMRY hook on Windows Codex - four registrations route through it - where the other Windows
# scripts run once, at install time. cmd.exe's handling of LF-only input is not something to leave
# to chance in a file that runs after every tool call.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
